{"kind":"report","text":"Approved: exact settlement ledger head 6c7ad6b64a5c111b51ce7d5710aba39b3681e8a4 on builder/settlement-ledger-20261104, tree 24d83fac, for request e13960b4 under promise 362f6644 and review request cdbf590b. This approval covers the six test and fixture files, the note and the two manifests. It claims no native admission, hosted authority or provider outcome, and no cause for 64552d40.\n\nScope. Main da94f450 is an ancestor, and the worktree is clean. Product source and dependencies don't change. fixtures-holds.ts gains only an optional ordinal argument on the Works constructor. The commit-message check passes for all 3 commits. No card path changes, so no Card run applies.\n\nWhat holds:\n\n- Ledger independence. settlement-ledger-model.ts has no import at all. It records jobs, duties, operations, requests, holders and settled items with literal fixture allowances, and it computes its own reservation and capacity refusal. The fixture adapter runs the real judges, fold, fits and reserved, and only projects what they produce.\n- Generated histories. choices() reads only the ledger, and the owner compares the full projection after every command. That projection covers jobs, duties, pending requests, operations, holders, settled items and the reservation. The adapter's write, repeat or scope-full result must equal the ledger's.\n- Named distinctions. Fixed prefixes cover pre-entry binding, ordinary finality versus settlement, capacity 20 versus 21, an unknown then late outcome on a final holder, a settled holder waiting on its step, the two-entry and four-entry allowances, and duplicate replies. Duplicate results and outcomes must return repeat.\n- Scope of the Works model. The note states plainly that a settled holder's histories start no new work and send no accounted request, and that the ledger doesn't rebuild graph reachability.\n- SQLite persistence. The storage owner appends each judged entry through a real SqliteStore transaction and folds it there. It reads the stored rows back and checks them against the fixture's entries and their hashes. It also folds the rows into a fresh MemoryState. Both the stored state and the replay must equal the ledger after every command, after a mid-history eviction and after a final eviction. This witnesses storage and replay agreement, not native admission, and the note says so.\n- Controls. The three edit sites exist once in fold.ts, draws.ts and request-duties.ts, and each control fails its named history at the ledger's snapshot assertion. The production hashes in source-identity.json equal the head's files. The helper copy differs from scripts/control.mjs on one line only, which keeps its scratch output.\n- The checkpoint trim. Between b8102c9a and d059b8df, the only change is one line: generated histories stop after their first checkpoint. Fixed prefixes skip that loop, so the nine named histories keep their exact commands, and the earlier control evidence still applies. The test-source hashes in the revision record equal the head's files.\n- The interrupted gate. The first gate at b8102c9a reads exit -15 and makes no pass claim. Its receipt lists the processes it saw during shutdown, and the follow-up record lists none. It names the owned group 26957 and each signalled PID.\n\nThe gate passed. At d059b8df, gate.exit reads exit 0. All five stages pass: kernel, 135 Node, 56 Counting, 3,811 plain with one skip, and 1,078 Native with two skips. before.json and after.json match, with a clean status and lockfile 5a717d43…. The receipt lists no owned process still present.\n\nEvidence. All 35 public files match their sizes and digests and are read-only. All four SHA256SUMS files match, no unit holds an unlisted file, and the credential scan finds no match. Both manifests name checkpoint d059b8df. The private inventories match on every file: 816 focused, 243 final focused, 2,275 interrupted gate and 2,276 complete gate with 39 selected. All are mode 0400. The note says that the earliest development logs are copies from /private/tmp.\n\nArchitecture: sound. The ledger states the fixture obligations as literals, and the tests compare it with the real fold, judges and storage at every step.\n\nSecurity: no finding. The head changes tests and fixtures only.\n\nSimplification: no required change. Two notes:\n\n- settlementProjection drops requests whose diagnosis is undelivered. That filter can't hide a request the ledger expects, but it would hide an extra undelivered request that production records.\n- The ledger's reservation rule restates the allowance policy as literals: two entries per held step or tidy, four per held request, one or two per open operation, plus one checkpoint entry. That is the purpose of a literal ledger. A later change to that policy will need the ledger updated by hand, and the three controls show the comparison catches a drift.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:3fda3393da5195d1a724445b6b789948e20e6a67","binding":"assigned","head":"6c7ad6b64a5c111b51ce7d5710aba39b3681e8a4","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:c1d34dfaa6eccc7161edfa830945892422209fea\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:362f66443304f9d124c60920dcc8c27529d3718f\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:c1d34dfaa6eccc7161edfa830945892422209fea","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}